### Describe the bug

When a variable references a function before it is defined, and that variable is referenced more than once, the first reference is inlined and the rest reference the original function that no longer exists causing runtime errors. If the same variable is defined after the function instead of before, it works correctly. Seems related to function hoisting.

### Input code

```typescript
var x = someFunction;

function someFunction() {
    return 2;
}

console.log(x);
console.log(x);
```


