### Expected behavior

```js
var x=function(){return 2};console.log(x),console.log(x);
```

It should reference the same function in both instances rather than inlining. This is the output that is produced when `var x = someFunction` is defined after `someFunction` rather than before.

### Actual behavior

```js
console.log(function(){return 2}),console.log(someFunction);
```

This inlines `someFunction` in the first reference, but still references `someFunction` in the second (which doesn't exist anymore).

